there can be few journeys more painful  to make  survivors of this death camp coming to  lay reads  against the wall where thousands were  executed  the decades have not dimmed the memories  how could they  more than a million people were murdered  here gassed or shot  men women and children starved  experimented on  and killed ninety-year-old rini saltz  thinks this will probably be her last  journey back to auschwitz  the camp where she thought she too would  die she is afraid  what happened here will be forgotten  i get very very nervous every time i  come here  it's it's not that it reminds me because  i never forget what happened here  but i come here because  i want to show other people what  happened  to explain to them what happened here  200 survivors made the journey to  auschwitz for the 75th anniversary of  the camps liberation in 1945.  some sharing their memories at a  ceremony attended by world leaders  and royalty the polish president  speaking from the very spot where jews  arrived at the camp by trains  what came to be known as the gate of  death  hatred chauvinism nationalism  anti-semitism assume the form of a mass  and organized methodical murder  he said the truth about the holocaust  should never be allowed to die  his promise to nurture the memory of the  victims stirring emotion in men and  women  already finding this day difficult  they were here to remember yes but also  to do what they could  to make sure history is never repeated  i hope you all tried to preserve the  memory of this place and other sites  where people  innocent people died i hope that you  will bear this responsibility  so that this terrible thing will never  happen again  many spoke frankly saying they won't be  here much longer  to testify to the darkest of periods in  europe  but they have left an important legacy  in words  and warnings michelle clifford sky news  auschwitz poland
 

